{"body":{"asks":"A source brings the world of ones and zeros into the field, but what was once called an integration went both ways: the field may emanate outward, as a REST endpoint from a structure it defines, a file of TypeScript types, a git repository virtualised from its commits. Is the source then two-way, and what is the interface, which integrates to the engine, beside it? The sixteenth session leaned so: a source holds things of its own and goes both ways, the one-way reading Knowledge's sentence alone, since the store's contract always took offers and the ninth session held integrations both ways; what the interface needs of the engine everything else needs too, the interface a primitive for drawing through components; a source owes identity of its content, history being its integration's choice; a reference into any source may resolve, where a type is asked, in place to a value of that type; and who runs a thing is apart from whether it serves or consumes the engine. Whether integrations and the interface are programs is the author's to hold.","awaits":true,"raised":[{"$ref":"1M7GJ08SSZN6051"}],"stands":"leaning"},"id":"1M7GJ0B2MPWP000","instance":["1M6YBY3G000001E"],"name":"Sources both ways"}